## Round 123, done

**The task** was round 122's first residual: the era-0 close-up of the Sahul crossing — the first crossing in the whole story — rendered as a near-black field. It reproduced exactly, and the probe found the frame was not night water at all but **moonlit dry seabed**: the shader draws era 0's shore 68 m lower, but the route planner's datum was still 0, so the voyage was planned across 200 km of shelf that the era's own picture shows as land, and the near-field sea discarded itself over every pixel of it.

**The mechanism**: `selectEra` sets the year, builds the fleet — which read its sea-level datum back from a shader uniform — and only then calls `onTime`, which writes that uniform. The router was always one state behind the picture. The audit's before-run caught it both ways: era 0 planned at 0 m under a −70 m picture, then era 1 planned at **−70 m** under a 0 m picture — Hatshepsut's Punt fleet had 34 track samples ashore in a Red Sea drawn 70 m too low, and Dilmun's Persian Gulf run was dry entirely.

**The fix** is one law with one source: the datum is `seaLevelAt(S.year)` at both call sites, and `onTime` now re-routes the fleet when the quantised level moves — which also covers scrubbing within era 0, where sea level runs −70 m to −22 m. The sahul record's waypoints now mean what they say at the era's own stand: "open water" sits in the Timor Trough at −1,159 m, and "Sahul landfall" sits on the paleo-shoreline instead of 200 km inland of it, both derived from the model's own elevation field.

**Proof and verification**: the new audit rules were proven on the live fault before the fix — 29 convictions of shipping code — and read 33 hulls / 0 problems after it. The re-captured close-up is finally a picture: two dugout canoes under way with wakes, in the water, a dim coast on the horizon. Opening ratchet confirmed r122 on all 59 frames; the close was targeted — globe-crossing, the only era-0 baseline, moved 0.004%, within tolerance.

**Recorded for next round**: the confirming second audit run plus the two staged injections; a `passage-sahul` baseline frame at a daylight frozen t (≈374 s puts noon on the Timor Sea); 23 tracks each carrying one sub-texel corner clip at straits and headlands; and the readout claiming "Nearest land: Ujung Pandang · 2 nm SE" in 60,000 BC — a modern port, at a distance wrong by three orders.
