## Round 124, done

**The task** was round 123's residual 4: the close-up readout claiming "Nearest land: Ujung Pandang · 2 nm SE" at 10°S 126°E in 60,000 BC. The row was stitching two derivations into one false statement — the number was the land scan's honest answer about the shelf two miles off, but the name was the port nearest *the ship*: Ujung Pandang, 528 nm away, on a coast the scan never looked at, and a port of the modern record besides.

**The fix is one law with one source.** The label layer already knew when ports exist (modern from 1900, historic from their own founding dates) while the readout did not, so that rule is now a single predicate both ask. `landward()` returns *where* it found land, the name comes from the nearest port that exists at the year and stands within 150 km of that found coast — otherwise the coast goes honestly unnamed — and the printed range never claims precision finer than the raster's own texel ("under 3 nm"). Measured before judging: the Sahul row now reads **"19 nm SE"**, unnamed, at the era's own −70 m datum; Yamato in 1945 keeps **"Kagoshima Ko · 52 nm ENE"** — the fix strips lies, not names.

**The owed r123 proofs all ran.** The full opening ratchet confirmed the routing fix fleet-wide; the audit read 33 hulls / 0 problems twice; the stale-datum injection convicts the two-shorelines arm, the steppe injection convicts the give-up path, and the new rule — "a gazetteer that names a place out of its own time" — convicts the old code re-forced verbatim. One structural fact recorded: the stale-datum injection *cannot* fire the track-ashore arm, because the audit samples the router's own mask and a track planned on the pinned mask is water on the pinned mask by construction.

**The owed `passage-sahul` baseline exists** — the sixtieth frame: local noon on the Timor Sea, camera pinned on the two open dugouts under way with wakes astern and no land in sight, which is the record's own row ("70–90 km, out of sight of land"). Three frames moved and each was read and accepted with reasons — the carrier's coast is now named by the port actually on it, the 1885 clipper's coast is unnamed, and a 415 BC "Soudha · 2 nm N" became "under 3 nm N" in a change too small for the ratchet's tolerance to see, which is exactly why the audit rule exists.

