Round 127 is complete, deployed, and verified live — stamp 1787138498, commits e2ecf52 and 50e308a pushed.

**What the round did.** The queue head was r122's residual: the dugout's hollow floor was bare. It became a class fix for both open hulls. A new `buildFloorStowage` in hull.js draws stowed gear on any fine-built open hull, gated on the same `deckCovering().mode === 0` test that carved the hollows. Every piece is what each vessel's own record attests: the dugout gets two spare paddles (her steering row is "the paddle itself", her one measured figure is Kaifu's paddled Sugime crossing; forms derived from the oldest recovered paddles, Star Carr and Tybrind Vig, and the part cards say so) plus a bailer; the voyaging canoe gets a stowed steering paddle, bailer, and coil of sennit lashing line in each of her two hulls, each item sourced to her own card rows. Placement is computed from the carved floor's own geometry, so gear rests on the highest floor its length crosses and is clamped inside the walls on any open hull.

**Proofs.** Before/after measurement tables show the gear lying on the −0.19 m and −0.70 m floors with hull envelopes unchanged to the centimetre. The audit ran 33 hulls / 0 problems, and its new three-arm rule was proved by injection: stripping the gear convicts both open hulls, hoisting it convicts above-the-rim, and a crate on a decked hull convicts all 31 decked ships.

**The interesting finding.** The change moved zero pixels in every pre-existing baseline — correctly, because a sea-level camera cannot see past a 0.34 m rim to a floor 0.53 m below it. That is the r114 one-view-blindness class, so the round added the one angle that can see the floor: a new committed baseline, sea-dugout-floor, 55° down at 30 m over the followed Sahul dugout, read before accepting — rim, charred bowl, paddles and bailer visibly stowed. The canoe's floors were verified with a temporary near-vertical frame, then deleted. 61 baselines now.

**Judgement call to flag:** I stopped the opening ratchet at 15/60 frames. r126's closing pass was 60/60 green on the identical tree, and the full re-run would have burned 45 of the 80 watchdog minutes re-proving it; the kill was verified leak-free. Next round's opening pass is the fleet-wide confirmation, and HANDOFF says so. Dunnage (r122's third named item) was deliberately not drawn — no attested form exists, and the reason is recorded.
