Round 128 is complete, deployed and live-verified (stamp 1787142504 at 05:34, well inside the watchdog). What the round did:

**The fleet-wide confirmation r127 owed: 61/61 frames green** on the unchanged tree — the largest mover anywhere was 0.043% of antialias dither.

**The probe-wake heading mirror (r114, carried 14 rounds) is closed, with a verdict.** The probe read heading as `atan2(dir.x, dir.z)` in a frame whose +X is west, so every course came back mirrored (360−H). Fixed at both read sites, and the probe now takes a frozen-second argument — at frozen=1 the Sahul track honestly bears 84°, at the baseline's frozen=374 it bears 195°, and comparing a probe read with a card requires standing at the card's instant. Verified against two attested courses: Ever Given 283.3 v 283, Sahul 195.2 v 195. The renderer is exonerated by measurement: wake pose and hull rotation share one yaw variable, the wake axis projects identically to the hull's waterline axis (0.00°), and the visible kink at the sterns is the hull's own seakeeping — pitch −2.3° and heave +0.5 m project ~3° of screen divergence at the 10°-elevation camera while she rolls 12.7° in a force-4 sea.

**The survey's vessel: sekibune (boxPct 78, head of the column) drew 31.0 m against her own card's 25, and panokseon 36.4 against 32.** The class: the loft grants `stemRake·loa + sternRake·loa` of overhang on top of the lwl, and nothing checks the sum against the record's loa. Rakes normalized to each record's allowance preserving the authored stem:stern ratio — planking now measures 25.00 and 31.98 m exactly, oars and rudder the only parts past LOA, which LOA rightly excludes. Profiles and both Shipwright frames looked at before and after: the sekibune keeps her raking stem, the panokseon her blunt bow. Audit 33/0; the two moved frames accepted with reasons; `action` re-checked at 0.003%.

**Handed to next round:** the class is 23 hulls wide (yamato +6.1 m, clipper +4.6, great-eastern +4.4…). The designed fix — a clamp in `hull.js` `rake()` scaling overhang to `loa − lwl`, plus its audit arm — was deliberately not applied: it moves ~30 baselines, and their individual verification did not fit the minutes left after the owed opening pass. Four hulls draw *shorter* than their record (wyoming −18.8 m); that arm is a record-semantics research question (sparred length vs hull length), and HANDOFF says so.
