**What the work is:** the rake clamp in `hull.js` — the fleet-wide fix r128 designed. The loft granted `stemRake·loa + sternRake·loa` of overhang on top of the lwl with nothing checking the sum against the record, so 21 of 33 hulls drew past their own cards, Yamato by 6.15 m. One scale in `hullSurface()` — `min(1, (loa−lwl)/((stemRake+sternRake)·loa))` on both branches of `rake()` — makes the rakes the shape of the overhang while the record's loa owns its size. The rudder's `postLean` now reads the clamped `H.rake(1.0)` so it can't lean past the post it hangs on. The audit arm shipped with the clamp as ordered: 'drawn length beyond record loa', proved by injection (30/33 convict on a 6% stretch, sparing exactly the three deep under-length hulls).

**What I verified fresh rather than took from the draft:** the audit re-run 33/0; the fleet sweep re-run — 0 of 33 over, every clamped hull at +0.00 to the centimetre, the under-length hulls untouched; the yamato and clipper profiles and four committed baselines read (trireme's card and drawn hull finally agree on screen); and the post-change ratchet's 34 movers cross-checked against FRAME-LOG's 34 accepts — identical both ways.

**The commit order changed on purpose.** The verified work was committed *before* the 35-minute closing ratchet, not after — r126's lesson applied: an accepted baseline with no commit is the most expensive thing a watchdog can destroy. The closing ratchet then ran green on the committed tree: 61/61, EXIT:0, largest mover 0.038% antialias dither.

**Handed to the next round:** the wyoming/preussen loa recut (their records carry sparred length in the hull's row — sources are in HANDOFF), container/slave-ship under-rake authoring, then the survey column at panokseon (boxPct 71).
