Round 133 is complete and live — stamp 1787591676 confirmed at https://augustg97.github.io/ships/.

**What this round did.** The survey queue's head item: the panokseon at boxPct 71, with the commander's tower flagged as 18 boxes. The open-pavilion branch of the tower code runs for exactly one hull, so the branch was the class. Looking at the committed frame and fresh profile captures showed why it ranked: four thin box posts, two rail lines running at the platform edge while the posts stood 24 cm inboard — the rails touched nothing — no way up from the deck the platform serves, and a near-flat cone roof that read as a pale smudge. The code's own comment cites the Gakseon dobon, and that plate draws a pavilion: railed platform, hipped roof standing clear above the bulwarks.

**The rebuild, each piece carrying the next:** four continuous round columns from the fighting deck to the eaves, an edge-beam frame carrying the floor, a balustrade whose rails run centre-to-centre and bury their ends in the columns (a first cut left a 14 mm float at the column face — the exact fault class this round existed to kill, caught by arithmetic before rendering), a gated forward rail with a ladder down to the fighting deck, and the hip roof at pitch 0.55 with eave overhang, hip rafters, fascia and finial. The record gained `towerProvenance`: the form is the plate's, the plan is read off it at ship scale, the scantlings are reconstruction and say so.

**Verification.** measure_ship reported the tower at twice its built extent — traced to the tool, not the model: `Box3.setFromObject` inflates the 45°-yawed roof cone's AABB by √2, and the r128 files show the identical inflation on the old cone (2.72·√2 = 3.85 exactly). Real eave corner: 2.15 m against a 4.70 m half-beam. Survey re-run: boxPct 71 → 63, the tower gone from the boxy-parts list, nothing floating. Audit 33/0. Ratchet: 62 frames, one mover — ship-panokseon at 0.229%, diff confined to the pavilion's own silhouette — accepted after reading, then re-checked solo at 0.000%.

**Next round:** the galleass (boxPct 70) and galley (68) share the apostis and bench classes, so one fix covers both.
