Round 134 is complete and live — stamp 1787596184 confirmed at https://augustg97.github.io/ships/ at 12:16.

**What this round did.** The queue head from r133: the galleass (boxPct 70) and war galley (68), whose apostis and bench classes are one code path in `buildGalleyWorks`, so one fix covered both. The committed frames showed why they ranked: the rowing frame was a comb — crossbeams drawn as identical sticks floating 0.6 m above the gunwale with nothing under either end, benches as unsupported slabs, no rowing furniture of any kind, some 200 meshes of it all plain boxes.

**The rebuild is the telaro the cross-sections draw** (Burlet in Morrison ed., *The Age of the Galley*; Guilmartin), each member carried by the one below it. The baccalari now cantilever from the hull's own rail — foot on the sheer at each oar station — up to the apostis, whose rail rides on their heads; they are tapered square spars. The banda, the stringer the bench ends rest on, is placed where the midships beam's top face passes bench-underside height, computed from the beam's own run — and since the sheer only rises from amidships, every other station notches deeper: nothing can float, by construction. A pedagna footboard stands ahead of every bench with its lower edge sunk in the deck — a scaloccio stroke is climb-and-fall, so a bench without one is not a rowing bench — and a thole pin stands on the rail at each station, bow side of its oar, the side the stroke bears on. The documented comment-vs-arithmetic trap appeared again: the bench comment said "outer end trailing aft" while the sign sent it bow-ward; the comment was right and the sign is fixed.

**Verification.** Measured in metres on both hulls: banda top = bench underside exactly (1.44 = 1.44 galley, 3.33 = 3.33 galleass), beam heads inside the rail band, tholes embedded in the rail top, footboards in the deck, and the galleass's gun-deck chain above untouched (stanchions 3.72–4.82 on the apostis rail, clamp 4.69, deck 4.94). Survey: galleass boxPct 70 → 39, galley 68 → 33, apostis and bench gone from the boxy-parts lists, nothing floating. Audit 33/0. Ratchet: 62 frames, two movers — ship-galley 0.194% and ship-galleass 0.227%, each diff confined to the rowing-frame band, action-lepanto and every neighbour 0.000% — both read, accepted with reasons, solo re-check 0.000%, exit 0.

**Next round:** the survey's remaining single-owner boxy classes — galleass gundeckx31, galley arrumbadax4 — and the fleet-wide grating class (52–70 boxes on nearly every hull; one lofted grating pays off fleet-wide). The impavesata (the rowers' screen along the apostis, attested for battle rig) is noted in HANDOFF as a record-semantics question before it is drawn.
