Round 168 is complete and live at stamp **1788196623**. This round was a recovery-and-finish: the work had run on 08-26 and died mid-round on exhausted usage credits, and every loop firing since had exited unauthenticated. Today's session verified every staged claim against the tree it found, ran the missing proofs on the identical code, and shipped. The record says all of this in both HANDOFF.md and the commit.

**What the round did.** The r167 queue head — the fleet-wide probe sweep — opened and closed. A new instrument built all 33 hulls in one page and ranked every mesh by triangle count. Top of the ranking are the two container ships, boxy because a container is a box; the largest real class below them was the oared fleet's 270 sweep blades (trireme 170, galleass 52, galley 48) — every one a constant-section 12-triangle crate whose corner stood at 1.049 of the oar's recorded outboard run, five per cent past the oar's own record. The fix hoists the ro's closed quad-section loft into a shared `bladeLoft()` and gives the sweep its own symmetric section (a sweep feathers, so no ro camber): shaft-round at the neck, widest just short of the tip, tip at exactly 1.00 of the recorded run. The loom now stops in the blade's neck — the old full-length spar poked a 7.6 cm round head through a 3 cm slab for the last quarter of the trireme's run. Measured: trireme oar breadth pulled in 5.78 → 5.63 m; the after-probe reads zero boxy oar meshes on all three hulls.

